---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="data-storage-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="data" from="gb" to="mb" mode="decimal" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert file sizes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>First choose how KB, MB, GB and TB are counted: 1,000-based or 1,024-based. This setting decides if the answer matches your operating system.</li>
      <li>Type a size into either field. Both convert as you type.</li>
      <li>The reference table at the bottom gives each unit in bits under the counting rule you picked, so you can see what the tool did.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The 1000 versus 1024 problem, explained once</h2>
    <p>
      Computers address memory in powers of two, so in the 1960s 2¹⁰ = 1,024 was a convenient
      approximation of "kilo", and the shorthand stayed. Metric prefixes always meant powers of a
      thousand. For a kilobyte the difference is 2.4 %; for a terabyte it is 10 %, which looks like a
      missing part of your drive.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Decimal (SI)</th><th>Binary</th><th>Gap</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>kilo</td><td>1,000</td><td>1,024</td><td>2.4 %</td></tr>
          <tr><td>mega</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>1,048,576</td><td>4.9 %</td></tr>
          <tr><td>giga</td><td>1,000,000,000</td><td>1,073,741,824</td><td>7.4 %</td></tr>
          <tr><td>tera</td><td>10¹²</td><td>1,099,511,627,776</td><td>10.0 %</td></tr>
          <tr><td>peta</td><td>10¹⁵</td><td>1,125,899,906,842,624</td><td>12.6 %</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Who uses which</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Counts in 1,000s</th><th>Counts in 1,024s</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hard drive and SSD manufacturers</td><td>Windows File Explorer and Task Manager</td></tr>
          <tr><td>macOS and iOS since 2009</td><td>RAM capacities, always</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ubuntu and most Linux desktops</td><td>Linux command-line tools by default</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Network speeds and ISP plans</td><td>Most older software and BIOS screens</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The SI and IEC standards</td><td>The JEDEC memory standard</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      For this reason a drive sold as 1 TB shows as 931 GB in Windows. The manufacturer supplied
      1,000,000,000,000 bytes, as the label says. Windows divides by 1,024 three times, gets 931.32, and
      calls it "GB" when it means GiB. Nothing is missing; the two sides use the same three letters for
      different numbers.
    </p>

    <h2>The unambiguous units: KiB, MiB, GiB</h2>
    <p>
      The IEC introduced binary prefixes in 1998 to end this confusion. A kibibyte (KiB) is always 1,024
      bytes, a mebibyte (MiB) always 1,048,576, a gibibyte (GiB) always 1,073,741,824. Their meaning
      never changes, so the list above always has them, in either counting mode. Use them in
      documentation or a specification to prevent misunderstanding.
    </p>

    <h2>Bits versus bytes, and internet speeds</h2>
    <p>
      A byte is eight bits. Storage is given in bytes with a capital B; network speed is given in bits
      with a lowercase b. That one letter is a factor of eight. For this reason a "100 Mbps" connection
      downloads at a maximum of 12.5 MB/s, and in practice a little less, because protocol overhead uses
      a few per cent.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Line speed</th><th>Best case download</th><th>Time for a 5 GB file</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10 Mbps</td><td>1.25 MB/s</td><td>1 h 7 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50 Mbps</td><td>6.25 MB/s</td><td>13 min</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100 Mbps</td><td>12.5 MB/s</td><td>6 min 40 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>500 Mbps</td><td>62.5 MB/s</td><td>1 min 20 s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 Gbps</td><td>125 MB/s</td><td>40 s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
